# ABC script for the liberty-mapped flow, passed by `abc -script`.
#
# The first twelve lines are yosys's own default `abc -liberty` script,
# reproduced verbatim so the mapping quality is unchanged. Everything after
# `&put` is the addition.
#
# Yosys's default stops at the mapper. `&nf` picks a cell for every gate but
# always the X1 drive, and it inserts no buffers, so a net with a thousand
# loads is charged its whole capacitance through one minimum-strength gate.
# On a small design nothing fans out far enough for that to matter. On a core
# with wide broadcasts -- a result tag compared against every issue queue
# entry, a squash reaching every reorder buffer slot -- four such gates can
# carry 87% of the reported critical path, which is a statement about the
# flow rather than about the design.
#
# `buffer` builds a fanout tree, `upsize`/`dnsize` pick drive strengths
# against the same delay target the mapper used. This is the cheap two thirds
# of what OpenROAD's `repair_design` does, and it needs no tool this flow does
# not already have.
#
# `-p` is not optional here. Yosys maps the flip-flops itself with dfflibmap
# and hands ABC the combinational logic alone, so every flop output arrives as
# a PRIMARY INPUT -- and buffering those is off by default. Without it a
# registered signal that fans out to a thousand gates keeps its whole load on
# one DFF_X1, which on this design left 6.8 ns of a 7.9 ns path in a single
# clock-to-Q. That is the same artifact the pass exists to remove, hiding one
# level up.

strash
&get -n
&fraig -x
&put
scorr
dc2
dretime
strash
&get -n
&dch -f
&nf -D {D}
&put

buffer -p -N 8
topo
upsize -D {D}
dnsize -D {D}
